summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Smith is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. The patient reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, so she wants to talk about that primarily. She reports that it is mostly in her elbows and it has been bothering her. She has been sitting at the computer at her desk more in the office, so she does not know if it is being stagnant in that position all day that has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. The patient reports that she is right-handed. She reports that she has bilateral elbow pain, primarily due to typing all day versus writing. She reports that she occasionally feels some numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time, and she shakes her arms out a little bit to kind of relieve that sensation.

The patient reports that she has a history of gout. The last episode she had was about 3 months ago, and she had some inflammation of her toe. She reports that the medication that I had given her really controlled it and she has not seen a flare-up since.

The patient reports that her psoriasis has been controlled as well. She was given some clobetasol for her scalp, and she reports that that is doing a lot better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral elbow pain.
Neurological: Reports occasional numbness in her hands.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
- General Examination: No cervical lymphadenopathy.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Tenderness and effusion over the right olecranon. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination of the right elbow. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.

Skin
- Examination: No lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

Autoimmune panel: Within normal limits.
ESR: Within normal limits.
Uric acid: Within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems.

Gout.
- Medical Reasoning: She is stable at this time.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to continue taking the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.
- Medical Treatment: Continue colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed.

Osteoarthritis of the right elbow.
- Medical Reasoning: We ordered an x-ray of her right elbow to see what is going on.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to her that we will refer her to physical therapy based on the results of the x-ray.
- Medical Treatment: We will refer her to physical therapy after we get the results of the x-ray. We also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day.

Psoriasis.
- Medical Reasoning: Everything is stable at this time.
- Medical Treatment: We will continue to treat her with steroid cream for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
