summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for an evaluation of back pain.

The onset of her pain began at a birthday party where she was surprised and fell down the stairs. She reports that she was crunched behind someone and jumped up when the person was surprised, causing her to fall down the stairs and hit her back on the railing. She states that she had a lumbar fusion previously. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. She notes that the pain is worse when she lies flat and when she twists. She states that she has a history of anxiety and takes medication for it.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.
Neurological: Denies radiating pain or numbness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: No focal neurological deficits. Positive with lumbar flexion and extension. Negative straight leg raise bilaterally. No radicular findings.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the lumbar spine shows a normal curvature with no focal abnormalities. No tenderness to palpation. No deformities. No focal tenderness. Normal range of motion. Normal strength bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine shows a normal curvature. No focal abnormalities. No signs of compression fractures.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today with back pain following a surprise birthday party where she fell down the stairs. Her physical examination was unremarkable. She has a history of a lumbar fusion. Her x-ray was normal. She denies radiating pain or numbness. She has a history of anxiety and takes medication for it.

I have diagnosed her with a strain of the back. She will continue to take Motrin 800 mg, three times a day with food. I recommended that she use ice and heat to her back, alternating. She should continue to walk. If her symptoms do not improve or worsen, I want her to follow up with me in one week.

All questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>
