summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Rebecca Anderson is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain.

The onset of her pain started approximately one month ago. She states that she has been taking up swimming more regularly. She has been trying to get up and get active. She notes that she started to feel some pain in her right knee. She notes that the pain is only when she swims. She denies any injury. She states that she is swimming breaststroke. She notes that she is able to do 10 laps a month ago but after a couple of laps she starts to hurt.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a history of carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago. She also reports a history of chronic back pain. She also reports depression.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.
• Neurological: Denies radicular pain.
• Psychiatric: Reports depression, history of depression. Reports that it is stable.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• MSK: Examination of the right knee: No swelling in the joint. Pain with range of motion of the knee. Pain with patellar inhibition.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee is normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Rebecca Anderson is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain.

Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Reasoning: This is a diagnosis based on the physical exam. She had a normal x-ray.
• Medical Treatment: I recommended that she take anti-inflammatories. I recommended that she do straight leg raising exercises. I advised her to avoid activities that put extra pressure on her knee.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that chondromalacia of the patella is a condition where the cartilage under the kneecap is soft and prone to injury. I advised her to avoid lunges and the breaststroke as it will put extra pressure on her knee.

Chronic back pain.
• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to swim.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue to swim. If her back pain flares up, I advised her to call me.

Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: This has been stable.
• Medical Treatment: Continue current medication.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue her current medication.

Carpal tunnel.
• Medical Reasoning: This is relatively stable.
• Medical Treatment: No changes.
• Patient Education and Counseling: No changes.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
