summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of hydrocephalus status post shunt placement. He has been followed by me since 5/2020. He initially presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. He was placed on a spina catheter protocol in 6/2020 and underwent Medtronic Strata programmable shunt placement in the ventricular peritoneal configuration programmed at level 2.0.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that he has experienced improvement in his walking ability and cognitive function. He is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, although he still requires occasional help from his daughter. His bladder control has improved but has leveled off. He is currently wearing a diaper. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper or any decreased or painful urination. He also denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is located. He has been doing well without any abdominal pain.

Mr. Watson is extremely pleased with his improvement since his shunt surgery. His daughter reports that he is able to have normal conversations without feeling lost, which was not the case before surgery.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
• Hydrocephalus

FAMILY HISTORY

• Sister with Alzheimer's disease

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

• Spina catheter

ALLERGIES

• Penicillin

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, unintentional weight loss. Reports improved ability to carry out daily functions.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Reports walking impairment, cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80
Heart rate: 64
Respirations: 18
Weight: 174 pounds
Pain: 0 out of 10

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Pleasant and cooperative male who is able to converse easily through his daughter adds some details.
Neurological
• Shunt site: Clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0.
• Recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge: Scored 26 out of 30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 out of 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. This is a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. At the time of the previous test, he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only one with multiple choice.
• Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a Gait score of 6-8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18-20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17 out of 28.
• Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination slow from nose to finger.
• Imaging: CT scan of the head on 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated there was no extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

RESULTS

CT Scan of the head 10/15/2020: Tiny subdermal collection in the right frontal region.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

PLAN

• Continue to monitor patient status. I recommend that we continue to follow him with repeat imaging and reassessment in 3 months. I discussed with the patient and his daughter that it is unlikely that he will regain the ability to drive at this time. He was advised that he could potentially enroll in a driver's safety program and that we could continue to watch for improvement.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
