summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on April 16th, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily, and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

She reports she has been struggling with not drinking. She wishes to discuss this further.

She states she last had a drink on Monday. She has gone a couple of days without it. She has been drinking for a while. She states she drinks hard alcohol 4 to 5 times a week.

She reports taking her blood pressure medications. She states she is only taking half of the lisinopril. She states the first time she took it, she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She states it is helping but she does not know it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking. She states she is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or shortness of breath. She states she is doing blood pressure checks at home. She states her blood pressure is 130 to 145/95 to 100 in the morning. She states it goes down in the evening. She states she is taking her medications in the morning when she gets up instead of the evening.

She states she has received her COVID-19 vaccine on February 9th.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, cough, shortness of breath, or sore throat.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea, cough, or hemoptysis.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or syncope.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or weakness.
• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety, depression, or insomnia.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• General: Well-nourished, well-hydrated, no acute distress.
• Skin: No rashes or lesions, normal turgor.
• Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No jugular venous distention. No peripheral edema.
• Neurological: Alert and oriented. No focal deficits.
• Musculoskeletal: No joint tenderness or swelling.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female with a past medical history significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. She presents today for follow-up of her hypertension.

Alcohol use disorder.
• Medical Reasoning: She drinks alcohol 4 to 5 times a week to excess.
• Medical Treatment: She would like to start naltrexone. We previously discussed this. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed the side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130 to 140/70 to 80. I will see her back week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection.

Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: She was started on lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide at her last visit. Her blood pressure today was 150/100.
• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will see her back in the week of 06/14/2021. She will receive her first dose of naltrexone on 05/17/2021. She is encouraged to continue her blood pressure checks at home. }}<|eot_id|>
