summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain and swelling.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He had a right knee steroid injection last time. He reports that the injection provided relief for about 2 weeks. He notes that he has been having pain in his right knee for the past 2 months. The pain is typically around 6/10, but today it is around 3/10. He notes that his whole leg is swollen. He also reports that he has been slowing down and has found ways to avoid putting pressure on his knee. He notes that the pain is worse when he has been sitting for a while and then tries to stand up. He reports that he is so stiff that he uses a cane to help him get his bearings. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He notes that the Celebrex was started 2 months ago. He also notes that he has been wearing compression socks, but it has not been helping.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.
Skin: Denies rashes.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Normal capillary refill in the toes. Pitting edema around the right knee and ankle.
MSK: Examination of the right knee: Range of motion slightly diminished. Palpation of the right knee reveals pitting edema. The right knee is warm to touch.

ASSESSMENT

1. Rheumatoid arthritis with bilateral knee involvement.
2. Right knee effusion.
3. Left knee effusion.

PLAN

1. Administered right knee steroid injection today.
2. Ordered an X-ray of the right knee.
3. Recommended physical therapy.
4. Discussed the possibility of needing a surgical consult if the effusion does not resolve with conservative measures.
5. Advised the patient to reduce his salt intake.
6. Advised the patient to prop his feet up when sleeping.
7. Advised the patient to try to avoid sitting for long periods of time.
8. Advised the patient to use compression socks.
9. Discussed the importance of following up with his primary care provider for routine Xeljanz monitoring.
10. Ordered CMP levels.

PROCEDURE

Right knee steroid injection.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>
