summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right upper quadrant abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Johnson is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. He rates his pain as 8 out of 10. He describes his pain as severe and squeezing in nature. The pain is intermittent and is associated with nausea. The pain is typically worse after meals, but it also occurs 2 hours after meals.

The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and has been able to control his blood sugars with diet and exercise. He notes that his diet has improved since his diagnosis with the initiation of a low-carb diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months.

The patient has a family history of gallbladder disease. His father and mother both had their gallbladder removed.

Regarding his exercise routine, he does not exercise around meals. He has not had any difficulty exercising otherwise.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Endorses low-grade fever.
• Gastrointestinal: Endorses nausea and vomiting.

VITALS

BP: 128/88 mmHg
RR: 16
Pulse: 68
Temp: Afebrile
SpO2: 99% on room air

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no ectopic beats, no rubs or murmurs noted.
• Abdomen: Bowel sounds are present. There is slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness. Positive Murphy's sign. No peritoneal signs.

RESULTS

Ultrasound of the abdomen shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bobby Johnson is a 68-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain.

Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has not had any improvement in his symptoms with dietary modifications. He has a family history of gallbladder disease and has multiple gallstones present in his gallbladder. He also has mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. There is no evidence of common bile duct obstruction.
• Medical Treatment: I recommend that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week for postoperative care. He will also follow up with his primary care physician for management of his type 2 diabetes mellitus.

The patient will avoid high-fat foods postoperatively and continue to eat a high-fiber diet. }}<|eot_id|>
