summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right shoulder injury.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Matthew Davis is a 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right shoulder pain. The patient states that he was playing tackle football over the weekend and was tackled and landed on his right shoulder. He denies any numbness or tingling in his right hand. The patient states that he is right-handed. He denies any issues with strength in his right arm. He reports that he is able to grab a cup of coffee, but he had difficulty picking up a box from the driveway. The patient states that he has been taking Tylenol for the pain, but it has not provided much relief. He states that his symptoms have been constant since the injury. He denies any neck pain. He denies any other joint pain. The patient states that he has a history of appendicitis with an appendectomy 3-4 years ago.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain. Denies left shoulder pain.
Neurological: Denies right hand numbness or tingling. Denies right hand weakness.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 122/76.
Heart rate: 82.
Respiratory rate: 16.
Temperature: 98.6 degrees F.
Oxygen saturation: 95% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
- Orientation: Alert and oriented x3.
- Cranial Nerves: Intact.
- Motor: Strength 5/5 in all extremities.
- Sensory: Intact to light touch.

Musculoskeletal
- Cervical Spine: No tenderness to palpation. Full range of motion. No ecchymosis or bruising.
- Right Shoulder: Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Radial pulse is intact.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right shoulder taken in office today revealed an acute AC joint fracture.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Right shoulder injury.
• Acute AC joint fracture of the right shoulder.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an AC joint fracture while playing tackle football over the weekend. X-ray revealed an acute AC joint fracture.
- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of his diagnosis and treatment options. I advised him that we will not need to perform surgery at this time.
- Medical Treatment: I recommend that we place him in a sling. I will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will also obtain a CT scan of the right shoulder. We will refer him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder and avoid future surgery.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow-up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
