summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Lower back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jeremy Lee is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes while his son was moving out of his college dorm. He rates his pain as 7 out of 10. The patient describes his pain as intermittent and notes that it is worse when he is sitting for a very short period of time or when he is carrying something heavy. He also reports a tingling sensation in his right toes, which he notes has resolved. He denies any difficulty or weakness in his legs. The patient states that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen for pain relief, with ibuprofen providing some relief for a shorter duration. He denies any stomach upset from the ibuprofen. The patient reports that he went to an urgent care center and was advised to obtain an MRI, but he was concerned about the cost due to his insurance limitations. The patient states that he has been trying physical therapy at his gym, but it did not help. He is concerned about his ability to lift heavy objects. He denies any urinary or bowel incontinence, and any other symptoms.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he had right knee surgery 2 years ago, which was a total knee replacement performed in 2020.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he is a retired teacher and a high school graduate. He reports that he is a heavy smoker, smoking 1 pack per day for the past 40 years. He denies any alcohol consumption.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that his father had a history of hypertension and his mother had a history of osteoarthritis.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills.
Musculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain, tingling in the right toes.
Neurological: Denies weakness, numbness, or incontinence.

VITALS

The patient's vital signs are within normal limits. Blood pressure is 117/56 mmHg, heart rate is 68, and oxygen saturation is 95% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Bony protuberance to C5-6.
Musculoskeletal: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Lower extremity strength is equal bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-rays of the lumbar spine were obtained prior to the patient's visit today and revealed no acute bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Acute disk herniation at other levels of the lumbar spine.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained that his x-rays revealed no fractures, which is consistent with his history. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate his current symptoms. I have also recommended that we initiate physical therapy. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once daily. The patient has been advised that if he has a herniated disk, we may need to consider further treatment options, such as an epidural steroid injection.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
