summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Shortness of breath.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and history of asthma.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports he enjoys biking.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking Norvasc.

RESULTS

Hemoglobin A1c 7.0.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Dennis Smith is a 57-year-old male who presents today for evaluation of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. He reports he has been coughing up blood for the past 3 weeks and has had difficulty with his blood sugar levels. The patient states that he has been having trouble with his blood sugars running high, especially after meals. He has been taking metformin. He reports that his blood sugars have been running high, as he has been waking up to 150 and after meals to 200. He has been taking Norvasc, but he has been taking it intermittently as his blood pressure has been running low.

On physical examination, he has no JVD, no carotid bruits, no swelling, and no ecchymosis. He has rales and rhonchi on more on the right side, diminished breath sounds on the right side, 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, which is unchanged from before. He has 1+ nonpitting edema on both of his lower extremities. He denies Homan's sign. He has no calf tenderness.

The patient will be referred to the emergency department for blood testing and a chest X-ray. A CT scan of the chest will also be obtained. We will discuss further treatment once we have the results of these tests.

For his diabetes, he will be placed on 750 mg of metformin twice a day. We will order another hemoglobin A1c today.

For his blood pressure, he will hold Norvasc for now. We will have him come back once we have the results of his chest X-ray and CT scan.

All questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>
