Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 57-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. She has been experiencing this pain for the past 4 days. The pain is located on the medial aspect of her right elbow. She states the pain radiates down her arm and along the inside of her arm. The patient denies that she hit her elbow on anything. She has a history of hypertension and diabetes, for which she is taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily and Metformin 500 mg twice a day. She states she has been compliant with her medications, but admits to sometimes forgetting to take them. She reports that her blood pressures have been in the 130s over 80s and she has been able to keep her blood sugars in the 150s. The patient states she has been taking ibuprofen for the pain with little relief. She reports that the pain is constant and wakes her up at night. She denies any numbness or tingling in her arm. The patient is an avid potter and baker and states that she has been working on a ceramic bowl for her son's birthday and has not been able to work on it due to the pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the right arm.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 120/70.
Respiratory rate: 18.
Temperature: 97.9.
Heart rate: 72.
Oxygen saturation: 100% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: No murmur, rub, or gallop. Regular rate and rhythm.
RESPIRATORY: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
MSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness to palpation on the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the wrist. No rash or bruising noted. No discoloration. No lesions. Pulses are 2+ bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right elbow: No fracture or bony abnormalities noted.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Right elbow pain.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right elbow pain. Her x-ray of the right elbow does not show any fractures or bony abnormalities. Her physical exam is consistent with medial epicondylitis.
• Medical Treatment: I have recommended a sling for her right arm. I would like her to apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. I have prescribed Ibuprofen 600 mg three times a day as needed for pain. I have advised her to avoid using her right arm for the next couple of weeks.
• Additional Testing: We will re-evaluate her in 2 weeks.

Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient's blood pressure was 120/70. She is compliant with her Lisinopril.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 10 mg daily.

Diabetes.
• Medical Reasoning: She is compliant with her Metformin but admits to sometimes forgetting to take it. Her blood sugars have been in the 150s.
• Medical Treatment: I would like her to increase her Metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I would like her to check her blood sugars daily and record them. I will order an A1c in 2 weeks. I will refer her to diabetic counseling.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
