Right heel pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Dennis Johnson is a 48-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago. The patient is training for a triathlon and reports that the pain is typically after activity, as well as upon first standing in the morning. The patient notes that after walking for about 10 to 15 minutes, the pain eases up. However, after prolonged sitting, he experiences pain when he attempts to ambulate. The patient reports that he is aggressive with his workout routine and is active 5 days a week. On running days, he notes that he runs about 10 kilometers in the morning, bikes for 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and then swims for 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. On his non-running days, he only swims. The patient denies any stretching routine.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

