summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 29-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The patient states she hurt her knee approximately one week ago while playing with her 5-year-old niece who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels. The patient states she heard a pop at the time of injury. She has been limping since the injury. The patient states she is unable to bear weight on her left leg. The pain is located on the medial and lateral aspect of her knee. The patient states she has been taking Tylenol for the pain. She is not allowed to take ibuprofen as she has an ulcer. She rates her pain as a 4 out of 10 initially and it has progressively worsened. The patient states the Tylenol provides minimal relief.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion present. Moderate range of motion. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was obtained and is unremarkable. No fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have diagnosed her with a strain of the medial collateral ligament of her left knee. I have recommended that we treat her conservatively. I have prescribed Tramadol 50 mg, which she can take twice a day as needed for pain. She can also take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have recommended that she wear a knee immobilizer for the next week. I have also advised that she ice her knee 3-4 times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time. She should avoid running for the next 3 weeks.

